  "query_preview": "..\n\nKey Message 1...Scattered showers and thunderstorms this afternoon \nand evening across western NY. A few storms may be severe with \nstrong winds and hail.\n\nLatest satellite and radar imagery showed widely scattered showers \nand thunderstorms developing across the Niagara Frontier around \nmidday as well as across southwest ON ahead of enhanced northwest \nflow moving into the region. Initial storms in Niagara Co quickly \ndissipated over the lake modified air with southwest surface flow, \nbut additional storms have begun developing farther east along the \nlake breeze boundary and subsequent outflows. Expecting an increase \nin storm coverage through this afternoon, especially further \ndisplaced from the lakes into the Finger Lakes region where \nMLCAPE has grow 1000-1500 J/kg. Outside of shallow low-level \ndirectional shear, hodographs are fairly unidirectional with \neffective shear 35-40 kt. This will be supportive of organized \nstorm structures and splitting cells. Any discrete storms could\nbe capable of producing large hail (1-1.25 inch), but the messy\nstorm evolution will likely lead to strong winds and heavy \nrainfall being the primary threat. PWATs are in the 1.3 to 1.5\" \nrange, which is above-normal, but not excessively high. Looking \nfarther southwest into Chautauqua Co, considerable cloud cover \nfrom upstream convection has overspread the western Southern \nTier. Additionally, forecast soundings suggest the area is \ncapped at the surface despite steep mid-level lapse rates near 7\ndegC/km. Any convection that develops mid-afternoon will likely\nbe elevated or delayed until this evening with additional \ndevelopment in OH as the shortwave passes through.\n\nHi-res ensemble guidance (HREF/REFS) suggest storm potential will \ncontinue into early this evening for the Niagara Frontier across to \nthe northern Finger Lakes region. The western Southern Tier may see \nstorms remain in the area through midnight. Lingering showers may \nremain into early Thursday morning before turning mostly dry \nthroughout the remainder of the day as high pressure begins to build \ninto the upper Great Lakes.\n\nKey Message 2...Mostly dry weather late this week with showers and \nthunderstorms looking to return Sunday.\n\nAfter mostly dry weather Friday and Saturday, a low pressure system \nwill track across northern Ontario early Sunday with a trailing cold \nfront that may bring the next round of showers and thunderstorms to \nwestern and north-central NY. The timing of this low is still quite \nuncertain owning to the ~50% PoPs lasting throughout much of the \nday. While the severe convective environment appears better farther \nsouth and west, latest model trends has begun to shift the axis of \ngreater instability north into the western Southern Tier. \nAdditionally, lingering upstream convection from Saturday night \nacross the Midwest will be another factor. Afterwards, high \npressure will build back into the Great Lakes with drier weather\nfor early next week.",
